# States, requests and errors

A request, a stored setting and a physical result are three different facts. Never write one as if it were another.

<h2 id="wg-031">Separate state from action</h2>

Label what a device reported apart from what a button asks it to do.

<%= example('wg-state-action') %>

<h3 id="wg-032">Say a request was sent, not that it worked</h3>

Say what you saw, not what you hope happened.

<%= example('wg-command-progress') %>

<h3 id="wg-044">Explain pending and uncertain outcomes</h3>

Say the request may still complete, and warn before a retry that could repeat it.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Garage door failed. Try again. | The garage door has not reported a new state. It may still be moving. Check it before you send another request. |

<h2 id="wg-033">Keep unavailable, unknown, stale and offline apart</h2>

Use the state the source gave, with a time. Say “offline” only if you know the connection is lost.

<%= example('wg-availability') %>

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Hall sensor offline. | Hall sensor unavailable since 18:42. Home Assistant cannot get its state. |

<h2 id="wg-034">Explain what the control can and cannot do</h2>

Show only supported actions. Say why one is missing and who can change that.

<%= example('wg-permission') %>

<h2 id="wg-035">Keep saved, enabled and running apart</h2>

Say whether a change was saved, will start automatically and is running now.

<%= example('wg-automation-status') %>

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Disabled. | Hall lights automation turned off. It will not start again until you turn it on. |

<h2 id="wg-036">Say what Cancel, Stop and Delete do</h2>

Name the thing and say what stops, continues or is lost. Say “you can undo this” only when the system can undo it.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Are you sure? | Delete the automation “Porch light at sunset”? This cannot be undone. |
| Stop? | Stop the vacuum? It will stay where it stops. |

<h2 id="wg-038">Make empty and missing states clear</h2>

Tell “nothing found” from “nothing exists yet” from “could not load”.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| No data | No devices match these filters. |
| No data | Devices could not be loaded. |
| No data | No devices have been added yet. |

<h2 id="wg-040">Give a recovery that matches the cause</h2>

Name the confirmed cause and offer one step that fits it.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Failed. Please restart your router. | The heating hub did not answer. Wait a minute, then save the schedule again. |

<h3 id="wg-041">Tell a mistake from a fault</h3>

If the person can fix it, say what to fix. If the system failed, say so.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Invalid input. | Enter a temperature between 5°C and 30°C. |

<h2 id="wg-042">Choose urgency from evidence</h2>

Use alarm wording only for a real alarm. A reminder is not an emergency.

<%= example('wg-warning-context') %>

<h3 id="write-a-warning">Write a warning</h3>

Put it before the step it affects. Say the hazard, what happens and what to do. Use “Danger”, “Warning” and “Caution” only for risks to people.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Be careful with the cover. | Warning: mains electricity. You could get an electric shock. Turn off the socket at the wall before you open the cover. |

<h3 id="choose-a-toast-an-alert-or-a-repair">Choose a toast, an alert or a repair</h3>

A toast disappears, so never put a needed fix in it. An alert stays until the problem is gone. A repair gives the cause and the fix as numbered steps.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| (Toast) Hub offline. Restart the router. | (Repair) Hub offline. 1. Check the hub has power. 2. Check it is connected to your network. 3. Wait a minute, then check again. |

<h2 id="wg-043">Stay inside what the system can know</h2>

Do not give safety, medical or legal advice the system cannot support.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Your home is safe. | No smoke alarms reported smoke in the last hour. This does not mean the house is safe. |
